1. a priority is always realisable as an exchange rate
   1200 lexicographic sections over 200 random 4x5x3 models, one per coordinate permutation
   realisable by a strictly positive weighting: 1200 of 1200
   no failures. So a canon that states which measurement outranks
   which has said something a weighting can implement, and the two
   formalisms are not rivals at that level.

2. the converse fails, on the real carrier table
   coordinate permutations: 6, distinct sections they produce: 4
   sections realisable by a strictly positive weighting (p2, L4): 58
   of the priority sections, in L4: 4 of 4
   sections a weighting reaches and NO priority order reaches: 54
   So naming which measurement outranks which fixes at most a handful
   of the available behaviours. The rest need an actual rate.

   the priority sections, named
     time > bytes > spread        -> ['d32', 'd32', 'd16', 'd16', 'd16', 'd16-control']
     time > spread > bytes        -> ['d32', 'd32', 'd16', 'd16', 'd16', 'd16-control']
     bytes > time > spread        -> ['packed-simd', 'packed-simd', 'packed-simd', 'packed-simd', 'packed-simd', 'packed-simd']
     bytes > spread > time        -> ['packed', 'packed-simd', 'packed', 'packed', 'packed', 'packed-simd']
     spread > time > bytes        -> ['d16-control', 'd32', 'packed', 'packed', 'packed', 'packed-simd']
     spread > bytes > time        -> ['d16-control', 'd32', 'packed', 'packed', 'packed', 'packed-simd']

3. three sections a weighting reaches that no priority does
     ['d16-control', 'd16', 'd16-control', 'd16-control', 'd16-control', 'd16-control']
     ['d16-control', 'd16', 'd16-control', 'packed-simd', 'd16-control', 'd16-control']
     ['d16-control', 'd16', 'packed', 'packed', 'd16-control', 'd16-control']

   and one of them, with a weight that realises it, to show the rate
     section ['d16-control', 'd16', 'd16-control', 'd16-control', 'd16-control', 'd16-control']
     realised by w = (632652750726840498046808184358179504921288462279128458437552756038472908509877615991897542390958031886090240/129596888071243600131351415941227052800468183212587584997608369044561133438602086960586955074250559896348806569, 285933337207686281787129485050226384546351426191902673416735509796273351329684965811174600439104602245738313013/302392738832901733639819970529529789867759094162704364994419527770642644690071536241369561839917973091480548661, 44949635620558472071743799147402959429773984676451175523989184630838569721590567978641601405703406314024075264/907178216498705200919459911588589369603277282488113094983258583311927934070214608724108685519753919274441645983) on (time, bytes, spread)
     check, by recomputing the argmin under that weight: True

4. a threshold rule has no weighting at all
   alternatives, on (primary, bounded): A = (0, 4), B = (10, 0), C = (5, 3)
   rule: minimise the primary subject to the bounded coordinate <= 3
   admitted by the bound: ['B', 'C'], of which the rule picks C
   choosing A: realisable by a weighting? positive
   choosing B: realisable by a weighting? positive
   choosing C: realisable by a weighting? no
   the rule's choice C is not realisable, so a strategy stated as
   a hard bound on a measurement is outside the weighting model, and a
   canon saying 'a strategy is a weighting' has thereby said that no
   strategy is a hard bound. That is a real consequence and it should
   be said out loud rather than discovered later.
